You are correct in your thinking. The `normalize("NFD")` function decomposes composed characters into their base characters and combining characters. The base character for "É" is "E", so if you call `"É".normalize("NFD")`, you will get "E" followed by a combining acute accent. 

Given this behavior, when you call `startsWith("E")` on the decomposed string, it will indeed return `true`. So, you can skip the `replace` step if all you need to do is a `startsWith` check:

```js
const firstLetter = this.selectedCreatorFilter.toUpperCase();

displayedCreator =
  this.model.creators.find(creator => 
    creator.normalize("NFD").toUpperCase().startsWith(firstLetter)
  ) ?? displayedCreator;
```

However, be aware that if you need to do other string comparisons or operations, the combining characters may cause unexpected results. If you need to do more than just a `startsWith` check, you may want to remove the combining characters to avoid potential issues.

